[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The access network and switch ports between Laptop2 and Laptop3 are healthy. Evidence: both NICs are up with link detected at 10000Mb/s, full duplex (ethtool on laptop2/laptop3 eth0). Both hosts have correct IPs on the same /24 (172.16.10.12 and 172.16.10.13) with routes present. Crucially, end-to-end tests succeed both ways: laptop2 -> 172.16.10.13 and laptop3 -> 172.16.10.12 each return 3/3 ICMP replies (0% loss, ~0.5–1.1 ms RTT). This rules out a dead port or switching failure between their desks.

Mechanism: The reported “can’t see each other” typically refers to file-sharing discovery/browsing (SMB/NetBIOS/mDNS/LLMNR), which depends on endpoint services and local firewall rules, not just basic IP reachability. Those discovery protocols or the SMB service can be disabled or blocked while ping still works, making peers disappear from “Network”/browser lists even though L3 connectivity is fine.

Localization: Endpoint layer on Laptop2/Laptop3 (file-sharing service and/or host firewall), not switch1 or its ports fa3/fa4.

Root cause: No network fault; the failure to “see” each other is due to application-layer file sharing/discovery being disabled or filtered on the laptops.

Corrective fix: No switch restart needed. On both laptops, ensure file sharing is enabled and listening, and allow SMB/NetBIOS through the host firewall (UDP 137/138, TCP 139/445; also mDNS/LLMNR if used). Restart the SMB/File & Printer Sharing service, then test accessing by IP (e.g., \\172.16.10.13\share or smb://172.16.10.12). If policy changed “this morning,” coordinate with desktop support to revert/adjust endpoint firewall or discovery settings. === END ===